solution:
The resistance of a conductor depends on:

- its **length** \(l\) — resistance is directly proportional to length;
- its **area of cross-section** \(A\) — resistance is inversely proportional to area, so a thick wire has less resistance than a thin one;
- the **material** of the conductor, through its resistivity \(\rho\);
- its **temperature**, since both resistance and resistivity vary with temperature.

The first three are combined in \(R = \rho \dfrac{l}{A}\).
